   "source": [
    "# PYTHON TRACEBACKS\n",
    "\n",
    "Related topics\n",
    "- Debugging\n",
    "- Exceptional handling\n",
    "- The logging module\n",
    "- Testing\n",
    "\n",
    "We encounter errors when running python scripts.\n",
    "Python has an inbuilt way to let us know when things go wrong, but it can be challenging to understand without any knowledge of what these error messages mean.\n",
    "\n",
    "Traceback is what you see on the console when your code fails. \n",
    "\n",
    "This text will explain the details provided in the tracebacks to help debug. Understanding traceback can save a lot of time used in debugging.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Types of Errors:\n",
    "### 1. Syntax Errors\n",
    "These are about the structure and grammar of the code, preventing it from even starting to run.\n",
    "Syntax errors are easier to catch because they prevent code execution.\n",
    "### 2. Runtime Errors\n",
    "These errors occur while the program is running, hence the term \"runtime.\"\n",
    "Runtime errors are caused by issues that occur while the program is being executed. \n",
    "It could be due to unforeseen user input, a missing file, dividing by zero, or attempting to access an item beyond the bounds of a list.\n",
    "Handling runtime errors often involves thorough testing, error-checking, and anticipating various scenarios that could lead to issues during execution.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example 1: Simple traceback"
   ]
  },
