  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "67646fe046bbac69",
   "metadata": {
    "collapsed": false
   },
   "source": [
    "- Traceback Chain/block is read from the bottom to top(bottom up). \n",
    "- At the bottom before execution stops, you will find the name of the exception that occurred with some explanation, e.g. ***\"NameError: name 'q' is not defined\"*** as in the example above.\n",
    "- The recent line of code to execute and caused the exception then follows immediately above.\n",
    "- The traceback provides a chronological order of how the code ran from the beginning until the error occurred but must be read in reverse. \n",
    "- This is intuitive because reading from the bottom to top you are finding out the trace, kinda forensic audit.\n",
    "- Tracing back, you are led to where it all has begun, i.e., the first trigger or the first call to initiate the code execution but resulted in an exception.\n",
    "- From here, you can even tell what really triggered the errors. \n",
    "- If it is a syntactical error, then solve by following the rules of the language.\n",
    "- If it is a runtime, the real bounty hunt follows. \n",
    "\n",
    "The example above is just a simple function and a function call statement. \n",
    "In complex programs like desktop apps, personal projects or frameworks such as Django, the source code contains a lot of variables, classes, files, functions, statements among other python objects. When errors occur, the traceback can be a bunch but with the skills of reading through and interpreting tracebacks can aid debugging to provide error handling mechanism.\n",
    "\n",
    "***NB:*** REPL (Read-Evaluate-Print-Loop) and Terminal display traceback slightly different.  **ILLUSTRATION PLEASE**\n"
   ]
  },
